                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the brief`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The signed brief lands: six required outcomes, a <strong>96-hour</strong> envelope (down from a 140h scope — <em>44 hours must go</em>), nine of 28 core faculty free, and the quality committee's assurance-of-learning rubric. No design is suggested. Lay the candidate modules against the six outcomes to find the <strong>gaps</strong> (an outcome no strong module serves) and the <strong>redundancies</strong> (an outcome over-served while another starves). The two best change-leadership professors are already committed, so <em>leading change</em> is the real gap. Confirm the hours math and name the binding constraints — hours, faculty, coverage and audit evidence are <strong>one joint problem</strong>, not four separate ones.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Sequence and select`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the actual program: choose modules to fit <strong>exactly 96 hours</strong> and sequence them into a deliberate arc — <em>foundations → application → integration → personal leadership</em>. A coherent program is not a list of good modules; it is a progression where each block builds on the last. The popular finance module is 16h but two outcomes need those hours; the integration capstone costs hours but is the only place several outcomes are <strong>demonstrated together</strong>. Put personal leadership on Day 1, before there's any shared experience to reflect on, and you've built a playlist. Allocate the four blocks, set the sequence, and space them across the four months to support transfer.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Faculty mix & assessment design`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Two decisions in tension. <strong>Delivery mix:</strong> core faculty bring rigor and accreditation credibility but only 9 of 28 are free; practitioners bring relevance and availability but variable teaching quality. Lean on the two stars across most days and they burn out — your dean sees the utilization report. Default to practitioners everywhere and qualified-faculty coverage collapses and satisfaction variance spikes. <strong>Assessment:</strong> design how each of the six outcomes is <em>measured</em> — pre/post diagnostic, an applied project on the client's real transformation, a faculty-graded deliverable. An outcome that is <strong>taught but never assessed</strong> breaks the audit trail and forfeits 20% of the fee. “We covered it in class” is not assessment.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend to the quality committee`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present a 3-minute approval pitch to the committee chair, with the client CHRO in the room. Show full outcome coverage within 96 hours, a coherent sequence, a sustainable faculty load, and assessments that satisfy both the client's outcome contract and the EQUIS reviewer. The chair probes the weakest link: <em>“Show me where outcome four — leading change — is actually assessed, not just taught.”</em> Don't optimise the four KPIs separately — this is <strong>one program</strong> that must clear coverage, utilization, satisfaction and accreditation at once. Approve it, and the launch holds and the rival school loses the client; fail, and the launch slips into a late penalty.`,

                    r1_frame_label:         `How does the team read the brief?`,
                    r1_gap_label:           `Reading the coverage matrix, which outcome is the weakest-served gap to protect?`,
                    r1_hours_label:         `Confirm the hours math: the candidate menu scopes ~140h against a 96h cap. What do you tell the board?`,
                    frame_system:           `As one joint constraint — hours, faculty, coverage and audit evidence all bind at once; the matrix is the backbone`,
                    frame_content:          `As a content-selection problem — pick the best 12 modules and the rest will fall into place`,
                    frame_hours:            `As a scheduling problem — fit something into each of the 12 days and worry about coverage later`,
                    frame_audit:            `As a paperwork problem — get a design the committee will rubber-stamp; the audit is a formality`,
                    gap_change:             `Leading change — its two strongest professors are already committed, so the gap is real`,
