                    coach_default:          `Design decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_system:        `<strong>That's the right frame.</strong> Hours, faculty, coverage and audit evidence are one joint problem: every module you add pushes another out, every outcome you teach you must also assess, and the matrix is what the EQUIS reviewer asks for first. Read it as one system and the four KPIs can reinforce each other instead of fighting.`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>“Pick the best 12 modules” is how good programs become incoherent.</strong> The best modules in the wrong order, over the cap, with outcomes you never assess, still fails the audit. Content selection is one lever of four — start from the coverage matrix, not the catalogue.`,
                    coach_r1_hours:         `<strong>Filling 12 days is not the same as covering six outcomes.</strong> Treat this as a scheduling problem and you'll discover at sign-off that outcome four has no module and the capstone has no hours. Coverage and the envelope are a joint constraint.`,
                    coach_r1_audit:         `<strong>The audit is not a formality.</strong> With EQUIS pending and 20% of the fee on a post-program assessment, a design whose assessments don't evidence its outcomes is an audit liability the committee can refuse. Plan the evidence now, not at the rubber stamp.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed it as one system AND named the real gap — leading change, whose two strongest professors are already committed. That is the gap the matrix exposes and the one the cut has to protect. Now the design can be built around what's actually scarce.`,
                    coach_r1_finance:       `<strong>Financial acumen isn't your gap — it's your redundancy.</strong> The popular 16h finance module already over-serves it while leading change starves. Chasing the comfortable outcome is exactly how the real gap survives to the audit.`,
                    coach_r1_nogap:         `<strong>“A module each” is not coverage.</strong> A module that touches an outcome is not a module that develops and assesses it. The matrix exists to find the outcome that only looks covered — usually leading change, with its faculty committed elsewhere.`,
                    coach_r1_squeeze:       `<strong>You can't shrink 140 hours into 96 by trimming.</strong> Shorten every module a little and you get twelve half-taught outcomes and a capstone too thin to demonstrate anything. 44 hours have to come out as whole modules — decide which, and protect coverage as you cut.`,
                    coach_r1_ignorehours:   `<strong>“Reconcile the envelope at the end” is Common Error number one.</strong> Design the ideal 140-hour program and you'll spend Round 2 amputating it under time pressure. The 96-hour cap is the design constraint, not a final adjustment.`,
                    coach_r2_arc:           `<strong>That's an arc, not a playlist.</strong> Foundations before application, integration before personal leadership — each block builds on the last, and the capstone has somewhere to integrate. This is the sequence that supports transfer and that you can defend to the chair day by day.`,
                    coach_r2_frontload:     `<strong>You put the capstone and personal leadership first.</strong> They're integrating frameworks the directors haven't learned yet and reflecting on a shared experience that hasn't happened. A program is an arc — walk me through why Day 7 follows Day 6, because right now it doesn't.`,
                    coach_r2_playlist:      `<strong>Sequencing to faculty availability is how the arc dissolves.</strong> The calendar is a constraint, not a design. If Day 2 is the capstone because that's when the star was free, the directors integrate frameworks they haven't met. Sequence is a design variable — own it.`,
                    coach_r2_starved:       `<strong>You've starved a block.</strong> A near-zero foundations block leaves application unsupported; a thin integration capstone is the one place several outcomes are demonstrated together — cut it and those outcomes have nowhere to be assessed. Fund every block in the arc.`,
                    coach_r2_nospacing:     `<strong>Spacing is a design decision, not the client's calendar.</strong> Blocks spread across the four months let directors apply on the job and return; cramming or letting the calendar decide trades transfer for convenience. Decide the spacing deliberately.`,
                    coach_r3_prepost:       `<strong>That assessment design holds up.</strong> A pre/post diagnostic plus an applied project on the client's real transformation, graded per outcome, produces the evidence that justifies the 20% fee and satisfies the EQUIS reviewer at once. This is assurance of learning, not just teaching.`,
                    coach_r3_attendance:    `<strong>Attendance and a happy-sheet are not assessment.</strong> “The directors are senior” doesn't waive the audit — the reviewer and the CHRO both want evidence of capability gain. A satisfaction survey measures how the program felt, not what they can now do. Where is the instrument?`,
                    coach_r3_taught:        `<strong>“We covered it in class” is the audit failure at the heart of this.</strong> Teaching six outcomes thoroughly and assessing none of them forfeits the 20% fee and fails assurance of learning. Coverage in class is not coverage of evidence — every outcome needs a graded instrument.`,
                    coach_r3_overload:      `<strong>You've put your two stars on most of the days.</strong> They're also running three other paying cohorts this quarter — either they burn out or those cohorts get a B-team, and the dean sees the utilization report. How do you cover the program without strip-mining the roster?`,
                    coach_r3_practitioner:  `<strong>Practitioners solved your scheduling problem — and hollowed out the program.</strong> With most days practitioner-led, qualified-faculty coverage falls below what the accreditor counts, and the variance in their teaching is exactly what tanks satisfaction. What's your minimum faculty footprint?`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A spread roster is the sustainable play.</strong> No professor carrying more than ~3 days protects your other cohorts and keeps the utilization report clean — while still putting qualified faculty on the days that need accreditation credibility.`,
                    coach_r3_rotate:        `<strong>Protecting the stars entirely has a cost.</strong> Rotating in newer faculty saves the roster but raises pedagogy variance on the very outcomes that need credibility — and the accreditor counts qualified-faculty coverage. Balance, don't swing to the other extreme.`,
                    coach_r3_undermeasured: `<strong>Half your outcomes have no instrument.</strong> Three of six graded means three outcomes are taught but never measured — the audit trail has holes and the fee evidence is partial. Push toward a dedicated instrument for every required outcome.`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>One design, one story — that's what the committee approves.</strong> Showing the arc, the coverage-and-assessment matrix, the sustainable load and the audit trail together is the integrated case. You're not defending four metrics; you're defending one program that clears all four.`,
                    coach_r4_singlekpi:     `<strong>You led with one KPI and the others contradicted it.</strong> Maximise coverage, or satisfaction, or compliance alone, and the chair finds the metric you traded away. This is one program that has to satisfy all four at once — show me the single design, not the strongest slide.`,
                    coach_r4_wrongstake:    `<strong>Staking the contract on satisfaction and utilization drops the two that carry it.</strong> The fee rides on outcome assessment and the EQUIS audit on accreditation — a happy cohort and a healthy roster don't save a design the reviewer can't sign. Stake coverage and compliance.`,
                    coach_r4_facedown:      `<strong>That faces down the probe.</strong> Pointing to the graded change-project deliverable and the pre/post diagnostic — the actual instruments — is exactly the evidence the chair and the CHRO need. Outcome four is assessed, not just taught, and the design is approvable.`,
                    coach_r4_probetaught:   `<strong>“It appears in three modules” is the answer that fails the audit.</strong> The chair asked where it's measured, not where it's mentioned. Three modules of teaching with no instrument is the teach-vs-assess gap the whole assurance-of-learning cycle exists to close.`,
                    coach_r4_probesurvey:   `<strong>A satisfaction survey is not an assessment of capability.</strong> Asking directors if they feel more change-ready measures confidence, not competence — and neither the EQUIS reviewer nor the CHRO will accept it as evidence of the outcome.`,
                    coach_r4_probedefer:    `<strong>“We'll design it after launch” is a sign-off you won't get.</strong> The committee approves the design that exists, and the fee depends on a post-program assessment you'd be building blind once the cohort is already running. The instrument has to exist now.`,

