                    insight_approved:       `Committee approved: you solved it as one program — a coherent arc within 96 hours, a sustainable faculty load, and an assessment plan that evidences every outcome. The EQUIS audit trail holds, the 20% at-risk fee is defensible, the launch holds, and the rival school loses the client.`,
                    insight_auditfail:      `Assurance-of-learning failure: outcomes were taught but never assessed, so the audit trail has holes the EQUIS reviewer and the CHRO will both find. “We covered it in class” forfeited the 20% fee — the cheapest assessment design was the most expensive mistake.`,
                    insight_short:          `The design didn't clear all four KPIs at once. Coverage, utilization, satisfaction and accreditation still pull against each other — enough for the committee to send it back for revision rather than sign it off.`,
                    insight_overcap:        `You committed more than 96 contact hours: the directors will not give you the extra days, so the program as designed cannot actually be delivered. Coverage and the hours envelope are a joint constraint, not two separate ones.`,
                    insight_overload:       `Faculty utilization is unsustainable: leaning the two stars across most days burns the roster and weakens the three other cohorts they teach this quarter. The dean's utilization report will tell that story before the cohort even starts.`,
                    insight_taught:         `Some outcomes are taught but never assessed. An outcome with no graded instrument is an audit failure and undermines the fee evidence — the teach-vs-assess gap is the single thing assurance of learning exists to close.`,
                    insight_lowaccred:      `Accreditation compliance stayed low: weak assessment design or thin qualified-faculty coverage means the design isn't EQUIS-ready. A program the reviewer can't sign isn't approvable, whatever its satisfaction scores.`,
                    insight_strong:         `Strong position: coverage, accreditation and satisfaction are all healthy — the program is coherent, assessable and one the directors will value. This is the disclosure-grade design that protects both the fee and the relationship.`,

                    hint_overcap:           `You're over the 96-hour cap. The directors won't give you the extra days — cut whole modules and protect coverage as you cut, rather than trimming everything thin.`,
                    hint_taught:            `Some outcomes are taught but not assessed. Give every required outcome a dedicated graded instrument, or the audit trail breaks and the 20% fee is at risk.`,
                    hint_overload:          `Your faculty load is unsustainable — the two stars are carrying too many days. Spread the roster so no one strip-mines the other cohorts they teach.`,
                    hint_accred:            `Accreditation compliance is too low. Strengthen the assessment plan and qualified-faculty coverage so the design is something the EQUIS reviewer can actually sign.`,
                    hint_coverage:          `Outcome coverage is weak. Check the matrix for the starved outcome — usually leading change — and give it a funded module and a graded instrument.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — coverage, accreditation and utilization all healthy. Lock in the assessment-aligned design and defend it as one integrated story to the committee.`,

